1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/31887916/lars-sjursen-gilderhug<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Excerpt from Minnie Holtan's Memories==
2 CONT As I stated before, my grandfather Lars Gilderhus was born in Voss, Norway, September 26, 1802. He married Randvei Seim in 1844. She was born in Voss, Norway in 1820, and was 18 years younger than my grandfather, which made for a 
2 CONC good marriage I am sure. For when a man gets older, I am sure it is good to have a young wife who is willing and able to help when help was needed. Their oldest daughter Martha was born in Norway in 1845. By this time, there were 
2 CONC rumors in Norway that in America there was plenty of food. Food wasn’t so plentiful in Norway, and the smallest of crumbs wasn’t thrown in the garbage cans, as it is the custom of our day. I heard my grandmother tell that little c
2 CONC hildren would lick their fingers with their tongue to get the last taste of their food and many nights go to bed hungry.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1846 or 1847, they made their plans and decided to go to America. Nils Gilderhus had already gone to America six years before and settled in Dane County, Wisconsin (1839). Now that the decisions had been made, they had to make 
2 CONC plans for the long journey across the Atlantic Ocean. It would take many weeks to make the voyage in a sailboat, even if the weather was favorable. I heard my mother say it took the Nils Gilderhus family 13 weeks to cross the ocea
2 CONC n, and by the time they landed in New York, their food supply was all gone.
2 CONT
2 CONT (See sources tab for whole document)
