1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/13388735/charles_benjamin-erickson
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles B. Erickson was born April 14, 1841, at Ottawa, IL, and peacefully passed away from the activities of this life on January 23, 1931 in the home of Mrs. Hannah Knutsen, Dakota City, at the ripe old age of 89 years, 9 months
2 CONC  and 9 days.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forty-two years ago he went to reside in Minnesota and after staying there four years he came to live in Humboldt county, Iowa, and has continued his residence here for thirty-eight years.
2 CONT On March 5, 1863, he was united in marriage to Anna Nelson of Ottawa, IL. To this union nine children were born; two of whom have preceded him in death, one son and one daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT He leaves to mourn his passing, his beloved wife who for more than 67 years has walked by his side in true companionship and love, seven children: Mrs. Ida Jacobs, Long Beach, CA; Lewis of Hampton, IA; Mrs. Amanda Johnson and Gilb
2 CONC ert of Humboldt, IA; Mrs. Esther Winter, of Minneapolis, MN; Mrs. Jennie Jackson, Sioux Falls, SD; and Elmer of Albert Lea, MN. Also 28 grandchildren and 17 great-grandchildren
2 CONT
2 CONT He is the last member of a family of six children. He belonged to that rugged, hardworking type of pioneer who suffered the many hardships that prevailed in the middle-west during those early days and who helped to transform these
2 CONC  broad prairies into fruitful farms, and build a commonwealth which is now considered the most prosperous of any section of this great United States of America. He was a man of wonderful memory. His mind retained those early impre
2 CONC ssions of his young manhood days with great clearness.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a member of the Mason Lodge for over fifty years and was ever interested in all that made for brotherhood and fraternity.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a long life he falls peacefully to sleep, the tools of toil have for ever left his hands but there remains much evidence of his toiling.
2 CONT His loved ones cannot help but mourn his passing but their sorrow is not a hopeless sorrow for they have the Christian faith that "In the land of fadeless day, Lies the "city four-square" It shall ever pass away, And there is no n
2 CONC ight there. God shall wipe away all tears, There's no death, no pain, nor fears; And they count not time by years, For there is no night there".
