1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Friday January 15th, 1932
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. K.O. Strand dead
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother is gone! Word passed quietly from neighbor to neighbor Friday morning when it was learned that Mrs. K.O. Strand who had been ill for some time, had quietly passed away. For days and weeks the sons and daughters, so near and
2 CONC  dear, had cared for her and watched at her bedside, knowing that it was only a matter of a short time when she would no more speak to them, and they could no more minister to her needs. She too, knew that her time was drawing to 
2 CONC a close, and made preparation for that long journey from whence she can never return. Even thought death was hourly expected, the passing of Mother's spirit to the great beyond was a shock, and her death is deeply mourned by the s
2 CONC ons and daughters, all of who were with her. It is ever thus, death leaves a numbing pall on the bereaved although they have expected the grim reaper for days, and also know that any minute may be the last for those who are appare
2 CONC ntly in good health. Ingeborg Strand was born at Spring Prairie, WI, where she lived until the year of 1869, when with her parents, they moved to Mona, Mitchell county Iowa where she grew to womanhood. In 1872 she was united in ma
2 CONC rriage to Knut O. Strand. After their marriage they purchased a home near her parents where they lived and prospered, until in the year 1910, when they retired from farming and moved to Lyle, MN to reside. To this union, eight chi
2 CONC ldren were born, two of whom were called home in infancy and leaves to mourn her passing: Adolph Strand, Geo. Rust, Mrs. Charles Culbertson, Mrs. John Peterson, Mrs. Arthur Volstad and Oscar Strand. A brother and sister Andrew Fad
2 CONC ness of Velva ND and Mrs. O. H. Fadness of Rio, WI. Also fifteen grand children and twelve great grand children. The deceased was a faithful member of the Lutheran church and confessed a living faith in her Savior and Redeemer, an
2 CONC d with full confidence of meeting Him, she expressed her self ready to go whenever she was called. The funeral was held Sunday afternoon at 1:30 at house and at the church at 2 o'clock. Rev. Langehough officiating.
2 CONT * '''Residence''': 1895 - Mitchell, Iowa, United States
2 CONT * '''Residence''': 1900 - Stacyville, Mitchell, Iowa, United States
2 CONT * '''Residence''': 1910 - Otranto, Mitchell, Iowa, United States
2 CONT * '''Residence''': 1920 - Lyle, Mower, Minnesota, United States
