2 DATE 20 FEB 1905
2 ADDR
3 CITY Leland
3 STAE IL
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ole Rogne and Brithe Rothe arrived from Norway in 1844 as part of the Voss emigration.  They married soon after their arrival and settled near Leland, Illinois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brithe left Voss on March 30th, 1844 at the age of 22 and Ole left a month later at the age of 27.  According to emigration records,they left "Soge Lykken", or to "search for good fortune" which means to travel somewhere to find a
2 CONC  better life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole and Brithe came over on the same ship, the Orman (Captian:  T.O. Groth) which arrived in New York on Friday, August 2nd, 1844, having set sail 60 days earlier from Bergen.  They married shortly after arriving in America.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole and Brithe went to Ottowa, illinois, where they stayed at the home of Lars Erichson (Erikson) Lydvo (Lodve), Brithe's uncle.  Soon afterwards the bought a farm south of Leland, IL, and not long after that, another farm three m
2 CONC iles east of Leland.  There they lived for the rest of thier lives.
2 CONT
2 CONT All of Ole's siblings remained in Voss.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole and Brithe had 13 children, 9 of whom survived to adulthood.
2 CONT
2 CONT Voss gave birth to a large number of Norwegian emigrant pioneers to America.  During the period 1836-1914 as many as eight to ten thousand people left Voss for America, for a variety of reasons.  They, so to speak, created a "Voss
2 CONC  beyond the ocean" but carried in their hearts the picture of their beautiful home in Norway. -'' Brochure'': Voss Norway, Emigrant Anniversary Celebrations (1986)
