1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Letter from Nils to his father in 1842:
2 CONT https://www.gilderhus.org/the-red-booklet/letters
2 CONT
2 CONT Mentioned in an 1896 book about Norwegian immigrants:
2 CONT https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_First_Chapter_of_Norwegian_Immigrati/igxQAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&kptab=overview
2 CONT
2 CONT Donated land and helped build St. Paul's Liberty Lutheran Church in Deerfield:
2 CONT In 1851 – Nils Gilderhus donated land and Jacob Thoe was hired at one dollar a day to oversee the construction. Each member of the congregation agreed to donate seven dollars.
2 CONT https://www.stpll.org/?page_id=16
2 CONT
2 CONT Bio
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils was born in 1808.  He emigrated in 1839 and first went to the Fox River Valley settlement in Illinois, then to Wisconsin where he bought land in Deerfield township on what later became the Forger Thompson farm.  He became the
2 CONC  first settler in the town of Deerfield.  He married in 1843, sold his farm the same year and moved to what is now the Irwin J. Christianson farm.  In 1856 he moved to Dodge County, Minnesota and in 1881 to Nelson County, North Da
2 CONC kota.  His family was large with nine children -- Marta, Sever, Gunhild, Fostein, Betsy, Andreas, Anna, Gjirtrud, and Lauritz. 1.Marta became Mrs. Quale -- Mable Larson's grandmother.  2.Sever married late but had a large family s
2 CONC everal of whom moved to White Snow, Washington.  3.Gunhild married her cousin, Andres Lie.  4.Fostein married twice.  In his last marriage he had two daughters, Nellie, now deceased and Ruby, Mrs. Vaught.  5.Betsy became Mrs. Ford
2 CONC e.  6.Andreas lived in Aneta.  All of the aforementioned lived in North Dakota.  7.Anna lived in Minneapolis and died young.  8.Gjirtrud became Mrs. Lenvik and lived in Minneapolis.  She had two sons, Henry and Norman, both now de
2 CONC ceased.  9.Lauritz remained on the family farm in Minnesota.  He had four sons, Nordeen, Reuben, Donald and Tom.  The latter died in a farm accident.
