3 CITY Kasson
3 STAE MN
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Farmer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/46151699/knute-lee
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Bio from Holtan Family History Document==
2 CONT On November 2, 1872 Knute Lee, age 18 married Kristens Gilderhus, age 18 West Saint Olaf Lutheran Church. An interesting detail, ten years after their marriage, as mentioned in the Gilderhus information, on the northeast corner of
2 CONC  Section 9, Vernon Township was the Chilson Store and the Vernon Center Post Office. Both closed in 1882 and the land was sold to West Saint Olaf Church (this was just east of the Lars Gilderhus farm). A home was built there for t
2 CONC he church Klokker (pastor’s assistant and church caretaker). The well there was dug by Knute Lee.
2 CONT Where Knute and Kristens lived when first married is unknown. However, by the early 1900’s they and their family of nine children lived on the southeast quarter of section 30 of Canisteo Township. In 1908, Knute left his wife and 
2 CONC nine children for Washington state to claim a homestead. He did settle on land near Pasco, WA. He took a job working the railroad to earn money for improvements to the homestead. He did not return to or send for his wife and/or hi
2 CONC s children. Eventually, the family sent the oldest son, Martin to investigate. Knute & Martin later lived at Kahlotus, WA. Martin didn’t return until 1939 when he brought his father, Knute’s corpse back on the train to Kasson for 
2 CONC burial. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Info provided by Don Holtan)
2 CONT
2 CONT This story is corroborated by the fact that Knute is listed in Kahlotus in the 1910 and 1930 censuses and in his 1939 death certificate (see documents in source tab).  The 1910 census shows Knute and his son Martin on the same pag
2 CONC e but in different households.
